==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Indo-European|man-|hand|lang=is}}, which led to a Germanic base with a variety of meanings.  [[cognate|Cognates]] include Old Frisian {{term|mund||guardian, guardianship|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|mund||hand|lang=osx}}, Old High German {{term|munt||hand, protection|lang=goh}}, German {{term|Mund|lang=de}}, {{term|Munt||legal power of protection|lang=de}}, Icelandic {{term|mundur||a sum a bridegroom pays his bride|lang=is}}. The Indo-European root was also the source of Latin {{term|manus||hand|lang=la}}, Albanian {{term|marr||turn|lang=sq}}.

===Noun===
'''mund''' {{f}}

# [[hand]]

====Derived terms====
* [[morgunstund gefur gull í mund]]

