==Old English==

===Etymology===
From the {{proto|Indo-European|man-|hand|lang=is}} ‘hand’, which led to a Germanic base with a variety of meanings.  Cognates include Old Frisian ''mund'' ‘guardian, guardianship’, Old Saxon ''mund'' ‘hand’, Old High German ''[[munt]]'' ‘hand, protection’ (German ''[[Mund]], [[Munt]]'' ‘legal power of protection’), Old Icelandic ''[[mund#Icelandic|mund]]'' ("hand"), ''[[mundr]]'' ("sum a bridegroom pays his bride") and Icelandic ''[[mund#Icelandic|mund]]'' ("hand") and ''[[mundur]]'' ("a sum a bridegroom pays his bride").  The Indo-European root was also the source of Latin ''[[manus]]'' ‘hand’, Albanian ''[[marr]]'' ‘turn’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/mund/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
'''mund''' {{f}}

# {{poetic| lang=ang}} [[hand]]
# [[trust]], [[security]]. [[protection]]
# [[protector]], [[guardian]]

====Derived terms====
* [[mundgripe]]

